//! lotus/limit_env_hold.rye — ALES59: an attack/HOLD/release limiter — the breathing limiter given the one stage between its two
//! knobs, and the rung that completes the HELD dynamics trio. ALES55 gave the limiter (ALES49) a patient sense of time, driving the
//! hard ceiling from ALES52's proven envelope follower so the ceiling is approached over an attack and released gently. Yet an un-held
//! limiter still lets its gain jump back toward unity the moment a loud passage momentarily DUCKS below the ceiling — it PUMPS. A real
//! limiter carries a HOLD knob for exactly this: on a fall away from a peak, it pins the peak for a little while before the release
//! begins, so the gain reduction stays engaged through the duck and the level stays steady. ALES56 built that hold stage INSIDE the
//! follower (hold_env.env_step_hold) and proved it once. This rung spends that proof: it drives ALES49's hard ceiling from the HELD
//! follower rather than the plain one — the same limiter, now with the patience that keeps it from pumping. The gain math is not
//! re-invented (it is ALES49's), and the time base is not re-invented (it is ALES56's held env_step); this rung is exactly their
//! composition over public APIs, adding only the hold knob. It is the mirror-sibling of ALES58's held compressor (the compressor at an
//! infinite ratio, the target pinned to the ceiling), and with it the gate (ALES57), compressor (ALES58), and limiter all hold — the
//! held trio whole, the mirror of the un-held trio (ALES53-55) over one proven held follower.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES59.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260814-182307_fill-ales59-lotus-attack-hold-release-limiter.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_min/sample_max, max_clip, the range law shape; lotus/limit.rye (ALES49) —
//! the hard ceiling this drives over time, re-proven beside it; lotus/hold_env.rye (ALES56) — the HELD time base, its env_step_hold
//! advancing the same follower one sample at a time with the hold pinning the peak; lotus/limit_env.rye (ALES55) — the un-held
//! attack/release limiter this reduces to at a hold of zero, re-proven beside it as the zero-hold limit.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket,
//! no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the attack, release, and hold are counted in sample INDICES,
//! not milliseconds against a clock, and the ceiling is a magnitude in sample units. One held envelope step and at most one
//! multiply-divide per sample; nothing on the path can overflow, since the gain ceil/envelope sits in (0, 1) so every written
//! magnitude is at most the input's, which already fits i16. NO LOOKAHEAD — a slow attack lets a fast transient briefly overshoot the
//! ceiling before the envelope catches it (this is a gain-reducer over time, not a sample-clamp); a true brickwall that never crosses
//! the ceiling under any attack wants a lookahead delay line, named as a horizon rung, taken only on Keaton's word.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/limit_env_hold.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const envelope = @import("envelope.rye");
const hold_env = @import("hold_env.rye");
const limit_env = @import("limit_env.rye");

pub const limit_env_hold_version = "20260814.182307";

/// The attack/hold/release limiter's faults, one consistent name each: a ceiling outside [1, sample_max], an attack or release
/// coefficient outside (0, 1], a hold longer than the widest clip, and a span outside the samples. Each coincides by name with the
/// tool it borrows from — BadCeiling from the limiter, BadCoeff from the follower, BadHold from the held follower, BadRange from the
/// whole family — so a composer sees one consistent fault surface.
pub const LimitEnvHoldError = error{
    /// A ceiling of zero (would silence, not limit) or above sample_max (nothing could ever reach it) — refused, no write.
    BadCeiling,
    /// An attack or release coefficient outside (0, 1] — a frozen envelope, an overshooting step, or a divide by zero.
    BadCoeff,
    /// A hold longer than max_clip samples — no clip is that long, so such a hold could never expire within a span; refused, no write.
    BadHold,
    /// A span outside the current samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Limit `count` samples starting at `start` in place to the magnitude `ceil`, the gain reduction FOLLOWING a HELD envelope that
/// CONTINUES from the caller's `env` and `hold_left` rather than from silence — the carried-state form (ALES43's lesson), now carrying
/// BOTH the envelope and the hold countdown so the hold survives across a call boundary. Each sample advances the proven ALES56 held
/// follower one step (attack while rising and rearming the hold, the peak pinned while the countdown lasts, the release only once it
/// expires); when the held envelope sits within the ceiling the sample passes byte-for-byte, and when it rises above, the gain
/// ceil/envelope (in (0, 1)) is applied to the raw sample — ALES49's ceiling reached over time. With `hold_samples` zero the held
/// follower reduces to ALES52's plain env_step, so this limiter reduces to ALES55's un-held limiter byte-for-byte. Refuses BadCeiling,
/// BadCoeff, BadHold, and BadRange before any write; on refusal the clip and the carried state are untouched. Values only — never the
/// length.
pub fn limit_hold_follow_carry(
    clip: *timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    env: *i64,
    hold_left: *u32,
    ceil: i32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) LimitEnvHoldError!void {
    // invariant: the ceiling is a legal magnitude — at least one sample unit and no greater than the i16 rail. Before any write.
    if (ceil < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    // invariant: both attack and release coefficients are legal fractions in (0, 1] — checked by the follower's one proven rule.
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(attack_num, attack_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(release_num, release_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    // invariant: the hold is bounded by the widest clip — a longer hold could never expire within any span. Before any write.
    if (hold_samples > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadHold;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder).
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const cl: i64 = @as(i64, ceil);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const x: i16 = clip.buf[start + i];
        // Advance the proven ALES56 HELD follower one sample; e is the smoothed-and-held magnitude, in [0, sample_max].
        _ = hold_env.env_step_hold(env, hold_left, x, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
        const e: i64 = env.*;
        // invariant: the envelope stays a legal magnitude — never negative, never past the rail (the held step's own guarantee).
        assert(e >= 0 and e <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        if (e > cl) {
            // The held detector has crossed the ceiling; the gain is ceil/e, in (0, 1) since 1 <= ceil < e. e > cl >= 1, so the divide
            // is always safe (the zero-envelope case the gate faced cannot arise on the above-ceiling branch).
            const m: i64 = if (x < 0) -@as(i64, x) else @as(i64, x); // |x| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
            // Apply the gain ceil/e to the raw sample. m·ceil <= 32768·32767 fits i64; divTrunc keeps it strictly below m.
            const new_m: i64 = @divTrunc(m * cl, e);
            // invariant: the gain reduces, never boosts — ceil < e makes ceil/e < 1, so a smoothed limiter only ever leans down.
            assert(new_m <= m);
            const y: i16 = @intCast(if (x < 0) -new_m else new_m); // safe: 0 <= new_m <= m <= 32768 fits i16 by sign
            clip.buf[start + i] = y;
            // invariant: the written sample holds the input's sign (magnitude only shrank).
            assert((x >= 0 and y >= 0) or (x <= 0 and y <= 0));
        }
        // a sample whose held envelope sits within the ceiling is left exactly as it was — the gain reduction has not opened; the hold
        // keeping the peak pinned above the ceiling is what keeps that reduction engaged across a brief duck rather than pumping.
    }
    // invariant: a limiter changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

/// Limit `count` samples starting at `start` in place, the gain reduction following a HELD envelope that begins FROM SILENCE (a fresh
/// env of 0 with the hold unarmed) — one implementation shared with the carried form, so the two cannot drift. Refuses BadCeiling,
/// BadCoeff, BadHold, and BadRange before any write.
pub fn limit_hold_follow(
    clip: *timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    ceil: i32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) LimitEnvHoldError!void {
    var env: i64 = 0;
    var hold_left: u32 = 0;
    return limit_hold_follow_carry(clip, start, count, &env, &hold_left, ceil, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
}

// --- selftest: silence stays silence; a HOLD OF ZERO reduces to the plain ALES55 attack/release limiter byte-for-byte (the zero-hold
//     limit); BELOW THE CEILING is the identity; a loud signal has its magnitude reduced with the sign held and never expanding; a
//     NONZERO HOLD keeps the gain reduction engaged across a brief duck (it does not pump, the knob monotone); the state CARRIES — env
//     and hold_left both — so a span in two pieces equals the whole once; and a bad ceiling, an illegal coefficient, a hold past
//     max_clip, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name ------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. silence stays silence — an all-zero clip limits to all zeros at any legal ceiling, coefficients, and hold.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try limit_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8, 3);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;
    }

    // 2. A HOLD OF ZERO IS THE PLAIN ALES55 LIMITER — with hold_samples == 0 the held follower's fall branch never pins, so every
    //    sample moves through ALES52's env_step exactly as ALES55's un-held limiter does: the held limiter with no hold IS the ALES55
    //    limiter, byte-for-byte by construction. Proven by running BOTH tools on the same signal (the zero-hold limit).
    {
        const loud = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400, 32767, -32767, 1200 };
        var held = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&held, &loud);
        try limit_hold_follow(&held, 0, held.len, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5, 0); // hold of zero, real attack/release
        var plain = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&plain, &loud);
        try limit_env.limit_follow(&plain, 0, plain.len, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5); // the proven ALES55 un-held limiter
        if (!eq(held.samples(), plain.samples())) return error.ZeroHoldNotPlainLimiter;
    }

    // 3. BELOW THE CEILING IS THE IDENTITY — a signal whose every peak sits within the ceiling never drives the envelope above it, so
    //    the whole signal passes byte-for-byte regardless of smoothing or hold.
    {
        const quiet = [_]i16{ 500, -900, 1000, -1000, 250, -500, 999, -999 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &quiet);
        try limit_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8, 4); // ceiling at the loudest peak, real attack/release, hold 4
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &quiet)) return error.QuietNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4. THE SIGN IS HELD AND THE MAGNITUDE NEVER EXPANDS — a loud signal limits so every output magnitude is at most the input's and
    //    every sign matches, under a real attack, release, and hold.
    {
        const loud = [_]i16{ 6000, -6000, 800, -12000, 12000, -300, 9000, -9000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &loud);
        try limit_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1500, 1, 2, 1, 6, 2);
        const out = clip.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(out[i]) > abs16(loud[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded; // the gain only ever reduces
            if ((loud[i] > 0 and out[i] < 0) or (loud[i] < 0 and out[i] > 0)) return error.SignFlipped;
        }
    }

    // 5. A NONZERO HOLD KEEPS THE GAIN REDUCTION ENGAGED ACROSS A BRIEF DUCK — a loud sample, one quiet duck below the ceiling, then
    //    loud again. With INSTANT attack and INSTANT release and NO hold, the duck drops the envelope below the ceiling so the gain
    //    reduction disengages and the duck passes byte-for-byte (the limiter PUMPS — its gain jumped back to unity). With a hold that
    //    spans the duck, the peak stays pinned above the ceiling so the gain reduction stays engaged and the duck is attenuated. So
    //    the held magnitude is no LARGER than the un-held one at every sample (the hold only ever keeps the gain DOWN, the knob
    //    monotone), and strictly QUIETER at the duck (the pumping avoided).
    {
        const duck = [_]i16{ 15000, 300, 15000 }; // loud, a quiet duck below the ceiling, loud again
        const ceil: i32 = 1500;
        var none = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&none, &duck);
        try limit_hold_follow(&none, 0, none.len, ceil, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0); // instant attack, instant release, no hold
        var held = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&held, &duck);
        try limit_hold_follow(&held, 0, held.len, ceil, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1); // same, hold of 1 spans the single duck sample
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < duck.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(held.samples()[i]) > abs16(none.samples()[i])) return error.HoldPumpedMore;
        }
        // the duck sample (index 1): the held limiter keeps the pinned peak above the ceiling so the gain reduction stays engaged and
        // attenuates it, the un-held limiter has already released to unity and passes it whole — the hold avoided the pump.
        if (abs16(held.samples()[1]) >= abs16(none.samples()[1])) return error.HoldDidNotEngage;
    }

    // 6. THE STATE CARRIES — env AND hold_left both — so limiting a span in two pieces, the split falling MID-HOLD, equals limiting the
    //    whole once, byte-for-byte: the seam invisible, so dynamics can span and automate.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 14000, 14000, 400, 400, 400, 9000, 6000, 6000 };
        var whole = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&whole, &sig);
        try limit_hold_follow(&whole, 0, whole.len, 1500, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2);

        var split = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&split, &sig);
        var env: i64 = 0;
        var hold_left: u32 = 0;
        try limit_hold_follow_carry(&split, 0, 3, &env, &hold_left, 1500, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2); // first piece — split at index 3 mid-hold
        try limit_hold_follow_carry(&split, 3, split.len - 3, &env, &hold_left, 1500, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2); // second continues env AND hold_left
        if (!eq(split.samples(), whole.samples())) return error.CarryNotEqual;
    }

    // 7a. a zero ceiling refuses BadCeiling before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (limit_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroCeilingSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongZeroCeiling;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedCeilingMutated;
    }

    // 7b. a ceiling above the rail refuses BadCeiling before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (limit_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max) + 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.OverRailSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongOverRail;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRailMutated;
    }

    // 7c. an illegal attack coefficient (numerator past the denominator) refuses BadCoeff before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (limit_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1500, 3, 2, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadAttackSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadAttack;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedCoeffMutated;
    }

    // 7d. a hold longer than max_clip (no clip is that long) refuses BadHold before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (limit_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1500, 1, 4, 1, 8, timeline.max_clip + 1)) |_| return error.BadHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongBadHold;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedHoldMutated;
    }

    // 7e. an out-of-range span refuses BadRange before any write, leaving the clip untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (limit_hold_follow(&clip, 2, 5, 1500, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRangeMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-limit-env-hold: an attack/HOLD/release limiter drives ALES49's hard ceiling from ALES56's HELD envelope — each " ++
        "sample advances the held follower one step (attack rising and rearming the hold, the peak pinned while the countdown lasts, " ++
        "the release only once it expires), and when the held envelope rises above the ceiling the gain ceil/envelope is applied to " ++
        "the raw sample (the compressor at an infinite ratio, the target pinned to the ceiling). Silence stays silence; a HOLD OF " ++
        "ZERO reduces to the plain ALES55 attack/release limiter BYTE-FOR-BYTE (the zero-hold limit, proven by running both tools); " ++
        "below the ceiling is the IDENTITY; a loud signal has its magnitude reduced with the sign held and never expanding; a NONZERO " ++
        "HOLD keeps the gain reduction engaged across a brief duck (it does not pump, the knob monotone); the state CARRIES — env and " ++
        "hold_left both — so a span in two pieces equals the whole once (the seam invisible, the hold surviving the boundary); and a " ++
        "zero or over-rail ceiling refuses BadCeiling, an illegal coefficient BadCoeff, a hold past max_clip BadHold, an out-of-range " ++
        "span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched. With the gate, compressor, and limiter all holding, the HELD dynamics trio " ++
        "is whole; no lookahead; no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
